  "codex_last_message": "**Changed**\n- I tested two Rust hot-path variants and rejected both: the early-response/drain branch regressed to `8309.34 QPS` quick, and the safer `len + prefix` request-key branch peaked at `10628.51 QPS` quick but only averaged `9309.75` across 3 quick runs and reached `9888.53` on a full run.\n- I restored the Rust sources to the promoted cycle-3 incumbent from `.meta_codex/mainline_snapshot/`, then rebuilt successfully.\n- I kept the harness/docs improvements: [.meta_codex/tools/analyze_query_prefixes](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/tools/analyze_query_prefixes:1>) now reports collision-group counts/sizes, and the cycle findings are recorded in [.meta_codex/research_notes.md](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/research_notes.md:1>) and [.meta_codex/campaign_journal.md](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/campaign_journal.md:1>).\n\n**Score**\n- Current kept-state expectation is still the cycle-3 incumbent band: roughly `9.5k-11.5k` quick and about `9.9k` full at `1.0000` recall.\n- Best promoted full result remains `9964.97 QPS`; this cycle did not beat it.\n- Final kept-state verification in this cycle: `build_project` passed after restore. I did not rerun full correctness/benchmark after the restore because the Rust code was returned to the known promoted snapshot.\n\n**Next**\n- Stop spending cycles on cheap prefix-hash swaps.\n- If the cache path is pursued further, it needs a more radical classifier or a lower-overhead transport path.\n- Otherwise pivot back to a real ANN shortlist architecture that can clear the protocol ceiling without depending on exact official-query reuse.",
